To set up the workerqueue:

1. Have a database connection with workerqueue tables (general_worker_queue,scheduled_jobs)

   CREATE TABLE `general_job_queue` (
     `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,
     `queue_name` varchar(128) NOT NULL,
     `creation_timestamp` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
     `time_to_live` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',
     `last_action_timestamp` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT '0000-00-00 00:00:00'  ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
     `status` varchar(20) NOT NULL,
     `failure_code` int(3) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',
     `no_of_unsuccessful_attempts` int(3) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',
     `max_no_of_retries` int(3) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',
     `payload` mediumtext NOT NULL,
     `failure_data` mediumtext NOT NULL,
     PRIMARY KEY (`id`)
   ) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4;

   CREATE TABLE `scheduled_jobs` (
     `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,
     `cron_spec` varchar(128) NOT NULL,
     `registered_with_pid` varchar(10) NOT NULL DEFAULT '',
     `queue_name` varchar(128) NOT NULL,
     `time_to_live` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',
     `max_no_of_retries` int(3) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',
     `payload` mediumtext NOT NULL,
     PRIMARY KEY (`id`),
     KEY `pid` (`registered_with_pid`)
   ) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4;


2. Have a "workerqueue" directory in the "/conf" directory where the following files are present:
    - .env
    - workers.yml


3. Have at least the following entries in the .env file

    WORKERQUEUE_DB_HOST="[host, e.g. localhost]"
    WORKERQUEUE_DB_PORT="[port, e.g. 3306]"
    WORKERQUEUE_DB_USER="[user]"
    WORKERQUEUE_DB_PASS="[pass]"
    WORKERQUEUE_DB_SCHEMA="[schema, e.g. webXX_workerqueue]"

    MAIN_SMTP_MAILER_IP="[IP, e.g. 0.0.0.0]"
    MAIN_SMTP_MAILER_PORT="[Port, e.g. 1025]"


4. Fill the workers.yml according to the following schema

basepath: "../"
workers:
  [WorkerClassName1]:
    path: "[Filepath to worker1 relative to workerqueue directory]"
    instances: [No of instances (INT)]
    instance_restart_minutes: [No of minutes (INT)]
    arguments: {[Not yet implemented] }
  [WorkerClassName2]:
    path: "[Filepath to worker2 relative to workerqueue directory]"
    instances: [No of instances (INT)]
    instance_restart_minutes: [No of minutes (INT)]
    arguments: {[Not yet implemented]}


5. Workers may require their own .env files (usually from corresponding subdirectories of /config/workerqueue/workers)
   make sure you provide the required data (look for "getenv" in the the worker's respective JobHandler & Worker)


6. Set up a scheduled job in the system (CRON on linux, Task Scheduler on Windows) to execute the script in
   "/module/workerqueue/processManagement/superviseProcesses.php" with php71 regularly, e.g. every 2-5 minutes
   (locally with php[71] -q [script-path], not via network)


========


6. To make use of the CronSpecScheduledJobHandler to perform scheduled (timed and/or recurring) jobs, you can place entries
   into the scheduled_jobs table manually or programmatically by creating an instance of GenericCronSpecScheduledJob,
   passing the relevant data into it, then pass it to the "createScheduledJob" Method of the PDOCronSpecScheduledJobGateway


7. To handle a new kind of Job, you will need a JobHandler which extracts and validates payload (if necessary) and performs
   the actual work. Copy the thematically closest of the existing Handlers and modify accordingly. Then you build a dedicated
   worker (which you can copy from an existing one as well), and give it dedicated error/exception/interrupt handling to exit
   cleanly in the worker class (if the default handling is not enough), and append below the class in the same file the
   script part which is later called from the ProcessSupervisor, which gets environment variables, constructs class-instances,
   sets up logging and starts the workers loop

